#let p = problem.with(code: "MM7033", unit: "U4")
#let items = (
 p(1,"E","U4.1 Presentations",[Give a one-generator, one-relation presentation of $ZZ slash 9ZZ$.],[Use multiplication by nine.],[Its image is exactly the kernel of reduction.],[The presentation is $ZZ arrow.r^(times 9) ZZ -> ZZ slash 9ZZ ->0$. Reduction is onto and its kernel is $9ZZ$, the image of the first map.],[Verify exactness at the middle and right terms.]),
 p(2,"E","U4.2 Exactness",[Check exactness of $0 -> K arrow.r^i K^2 arrow.r^p K ->0$, where $i(a)=(a,0)$ and $p(a,b)=b$.],[Compute kernel and image.],[Both the kernel of $p$ and image of $i$ are the first coordinate axis.],[$i$ is injective, $p$ is onto, and $ker p=\{(a,0)\}="im"i$. Thus the sequence is short exact. A section is $b|->(0,b)$.],[Check all three requirements.]),
 p(3,"E","U4.1 Finite support",[Give an element of $product_(n>=0)ZZ$ not lying in $bigoplus_(n>=0)ZZ$.],[Use infinitely many nonzero coordinates.],[The all-ones sequence works.],[The tuple $(1,1,1,dots)$ belongs to the product because every coordinate is an integer. Its support is infinite, so it is absent from the direct sum.],[State the support condition.]),
 p(4,"E","U4.1 Free maps",[How is a linear map $R^3 -> M$ determined?],[Choose the images of the three standard basis elements.],[Extend by scalar combinations.],[An arbitrary triple $m_1,m_2,m_3 in M$ gives the unique map $(r_1,r_2,r_3)|->r_1m_1+r_2m_2+r_3m_3$. Conversely every linear map has this form.],[Explain uniqueness by basis expansion.]),
 p(5,"C","U4.2 Nonsplitting",[Prove $0 -> ZZ arrow.r^(times 3) ZZ -> ZZ slash 3ZZ ->0$ does not split.],[A section's image of $[1]$ must be annihilated by three.],[No nonzero integer is annihilated by three.],[If $s$ were a section, then $3s([1])=s(3[1])=0$, so $s([1])=0$. Reducing this image modulo three gives zero, contradicting that the composite is the identity on $[1]$.],[Use linearity and the section equation.]),
 p(6,"C","U4.2 Free quotients",[Show a short exact sequence with quotient $R^n$ splits.],[Lift each standard basis vector.],[Extend the chosen lifts linearly.],[Choose $b_i$ mapping to the $i$th standard basis vector under the surjection. Define $s(r_1,dots,r_n)=sum_i r_i b_i$. This is linear and composing with the surjection returns the input vector. Thus it is a section and the splitting lemma applies.],[Construct an actual section.]),
 p(7,"C","U4.1 Presentation computation",[Identify the $ZZ$-module generated by $u,v$ with relations $u-2v=0$ and $6v=0$.],[Eliminate $u$ using the first relation.],[The remaining generator has order six.],[Every element becomes a multiple of $v$, and the remaining relation is $6v=0$. The map to $ZZ slash 6ZZ$ sending $v$ to $[1]$ and $u$ to $[2]$ respects both relations. The reverse map sends $[1]$ to $v$, and their composites are identities. Hence the module is $ZZ slash 6ZZ$.],[Prove no extra hidden relation remains by constructing inverse maps.]),
 p(8,"C","U4.2 Direct summands",[Prove a direct summand of a projective module is projective.],[Extend a map from the summand using the projection.],[Lift through the larger module and restrict back.],[Write $P=A oplus B$ with $P$ projective. Given $f:A -> N$ and a surjection $M -> N$, compose $f$ with the projection $P -> A$, lift that map to $M$ using projectivity, then restrict along $A -> P$. The resulting map lifts $f$. Thus $A$ is projective.],[State the extension and restriction maps.]),
 p(9,"C","U4.1 Finite rank",[Prove $R^2$ cannot be isomorphic to $R^3$ for a nonzero commutative unital ring.],[Reduce modulo a maximal ideal.],[An isomorphism induces an isomorphism of residue-field vector spaces.],[Choose a maximal ideal $M$. Quotienting the two modules by $M$ times themselves would give an isomorphism $(R slash M)^2 iso (R slash M)^3$. Vector-space dimension rules this out.],[Explain why the module isomorphism descends to these quotients.]),
 p(10,"A","U4.2 Projective cyclic example",[Show $ZZ slash 2ZZ$ is not a projective $ZZ$-module.],[Apply its lifting property to reduction $ZZ -> ZZ slash 2ZZ$.],[Lifting the identity would provide a section.],[If the module were projective, its identity would lift through the surjection from $ZZ$, giving a section. Such a section must send $[1]$ to an integer killed by two, hence to zero, contradicting the section condition. Therefore it is not projective.],[Use the definition of projectivity with fully specified maps.]),
 p(11,"A","U4.2 A projective nonfree module",[Let $R=K times K$ and $e=(1,0)$. Prove $R e$ is projective but is not free.],[Use $R=R e oplus R(1-e)$.],[Every element of $R e$ is annihilated by $(0,1)$.],[The decomposition $R=R e oplus R(1-e)$ follows by splitting $(a,b)=(a,0)+(0,b)$. It makes $R e$ a direct summand of the free module $R$, hence projective. It is nonzero and is annihilated by the nonzero element $(0,1)$. A nonzero free module has a basis vector not annihilated by any nonzero scalar, since its corresponding coordinate is that scalar. Thus $R e$ is not free of any rank.],[Exclude rank zero and justify the annihilator property of free modules.]),
 p(12,
  "A",
  "U4.2 Splitting from a retraction",
  [Given a short exact sequence and a retraction $r:B -> A$ of its injection $i$, construct the isomorphism $B iso A oplus C$.],
  [Subtract $i(r(b))$ to isolate the complementary part.],
  [The restriction of the quotient map to $ker r$ is an isomorphism.],
  [*Finding the approach.* A retraction extracts the A-coordinate. Subtract that coordinate from a general element to isolate a complementary piece that maps bijectively to C.

*Argument.* The map $p:ker r -> C$ is injective because a kernel element is $i(a)$ with $a=r i(a)=0$. It is onto because any lift $b$ of $c$ can be replaced by $b-i(r(b))$. Let $s$ be its inverse. Then $(a,c)|->i(a)+s(c)$ has inverse $b|->(r(b),p(b))$. Direct substitution verifies both identities.

If $b in ker(r)$ and $p(b)=0$, exactness gives $b=i(a)$ and the retraction identity gives $0=r(b)=a$, proving injectivity. For a chosen lift of $c$, subtracting $i(r(b))$ changes neither $p(b)$ nor its target $c$, because $p i=0$. It makes the retraction zero because $r i=id$. The inverse of the restricted bijective module map is itself linear, so $s$ is a section. For the composite on $B$, the difference $b-i(r(b))$ is the unique element of $ker(r)$ mapping to $p(b)$, hence equals $s(p(b))$.

*A tempting dead end.* Choosing an arbitrary preimage of every $c$ does not ensure those choices form a linear section. The restriction to $ker(r)$ makes the lift unique and forces linearity.

*Hypotheses.* Exactness identifies $ker(p)$ with the image of the injection, and $r i=id_A$ is indispensable. A short exact sequence need not split without a retraction or section.],
  [Prove the restriction is bijective before using its inverse.],
  idea: [Subtract the part already detected by the retraction; the remaining kernel supplies a canonical complement.]),
 p(13,
  "A",
  "U4 Transfer: Lifting maps from a residue ideal",
  [Let $R=ZZ slash 6ZZ$ and $M=2R$. Given any surjective R-linear map $q:N -> P$ and any R-linear map $f:M -> P$, construct an R-linear $h:M -> N$ with $q h=f$. Is M free over R? Justify your answer.],
  [Find an element of M that acts as the identity on every element of M.],
  [The residue e of four satisfies e squared equals e and M equals Re. Lift f(e), then multiply the lift by e.],
  [*Finding the approach.* The generator two has a relation that an arbitrary preimage need not satisfy. The other nonzero residue in M, namely four, is an idempotent. It can repair a chosen preimage while leaving its image unchanged.

*Argument.* Write $e=[4]$. Then $e^2=e$, $R e=\{[0],[2],[4]\}=M$, and $e m=m$ for every $m in M$. Choose $n in N$ with $q(n)=f(e)$. Define $h(r e)=r e n$. If $r e=r' e$, then $(r-r')e=0$ in R, so $(r-r')e n=0$; thus h is well defined. Its formula respects addition and scalar multiplication. Moreover $q h(r e)=r e f(e)=r f(e)=f(r e)$, since $e f(e)=f(e^2)=f(e)$. This constructs the required lift for every q and f, and proves projectivity. However, the nonzero scalar $[3]$ kills all of M. In any nonzero free R-module, multiplying a basis vector by $[3]$ gives a vector with nonzero coordinate $[3]$. Since M is nonzero, it cannot be free.

*A tempting dead end.* Choosing any preimage of f([2]) and extending from the generator ignores the relation $[3][2]=0$. The preimage need not be killed by three. Nor does having three elements make M a one-dimensional R-vector space: R is not a field.

*Hypotheses.* Surjectivity supplies the preimage, R-linearity lets the idempotent repair it, and the arithmetic modulo six supplies the nontrivial idempotent and annihilator. The obstruction concerns freeness over R, not over a different coefficient ring.],
  [Check the lift's relation and linearity, and give an annihilator argument that excludes every nonzero free module.],
  optional: true,
  minutes: 60,
  idea: [Use an idempotent to repair a lift, and its complement to detect why the module cannot be free.]),
)
